+# Model routing: provider-scoped tiers, attribution and cost
+
+Contract (1.43.0). Mock2 defines the policy, the data contracts and the acceptance; each integrating tool's backend
+supplies provider connections, available models, tier bindings, execution adapters and controls. The orchestration
+tool (`.mock2/bin/orchestrate.mjs`, library `.mock2/bin/orchestration-config.mjs`) implements exactly what Mock2 uses
+to resolve and record its own dispatches. It never calls a model. A tool that does not implement a part of this
+contract says so; nothing here is a build gate.
+
+## Tiers are scoped to a provider
+
+A tier is a qualified key `provider_id/tier_key`: `openai/economy` and `anthropic/economy` are distinct and are bound
+independently. An unqualified key (`economy`) never dispatches; it resolves to the status `unqualified-tier`. The
+starting labels are `economy`, `balanced` and `flagship`. Each provider keeps its own mapping and its own tier set:
+it may omit a label or add a provider-scoped tier such as `anthropic/long-context`. No ranking across providers is
+implied, and a same-named tier on another provider never authorises a switch.
+
+A binding is configuration, never a family-to-tier rule. Concrete model ids live only in a versioned model registry
+owned by the host. Standard and rule text names no current model release; samples use placeholders. A binding is
+connection-scoped (an account, workspace or endpoint identity, never a secret) and never selects through another
+connection or provider. Adopting a new model is a registry change, and every execution records the registry and
+policy version and the binding it used.
+
+Effort, output limit, processing speed and required capabilities are settings, independent of the tier: a
+flagship tier at `reasoning_effort: low` is still `flagship`, and a fast processing tier changes only
+`processing_tier`. Escalation never couples a model change with an effort change.
+
+## Roles and the resolved route
+
+Roles: `coordinator`, `investigator` (investigator or planner), `builder`, `reviewer` (independent reviewer or
+validator) and `release-helper` (the release runner of PRO §P, with no promotion authority). A resolved route is
+
+```json
+{ "provider": "openai", "tier": "openai/economy", "connection": "<connection id>", "model": "<model id>",
+  "pinned": false,
+  "settings": { "reasoning_effort": "high", "max_output_tokens": null, "processing_tier": null, "required_capabilities": [] } }
+```
+
+or a visible status naming what is missing. Statuses: `planned`, `planned-fallback` (both routes recorded),
+`waiting` (with a reason and, where it applies, `waiting_reason`), `unqualified-tier`, `binding-missing` (the missing
+qualified key and the layers consulted), `capability-mismatch` (the missing capabilities), `binding-retired`,
+`unsupported` (a host restriction: unavailable model, unsupported effort, price class above the main model's),
+`external-runner-required` (a native harness cannot run that provider) and `disabled` (direct or off). Every result
+names its `resolution_source`.
+
+## Precedence
+
+Highest first: 1 an authorized job override (per invocation, or an explicit chat request at session scope:
+`session`, then `job`); 2 the environment's role configuration (`environment`, a host-supplied settings file named by
+`MOCK2_ORCHESTRATION_ENVIRONMENT` or `--environment FILE`); 3 `project` (`.mock2/orchestration.json`); 4 `user`
+(`~/.mock2/orchestration.json`); 5 the provider's role default in the registry (`role_defaults`, source
+`registry-role-default`). When no layer names a provider, the registry's top-level `default_provider` applies. A
+registry with no `default_provider` and no settings naming a provider resolves every role to `binding-missing`; no
+provider is guessed. The personal off switch (user scope `mode: off`) outranks everything for whether to dispatch.
+
+Settings merge per field from the layer that chose the binding upward, over the bound tier's `settings_defaults`.
+A tier binding chosen at a scope does not inherit lower-scope tuning made for another binding; a setting at the same
+or a higher scope applies. An operator pin has no tier defaults: it takes the settings of the route it replaces (the
+layers below it), then its own and higher settings.
+
+## Settings, schema version 2
+
+`orchestration.json` at user, project or session scope (and the read-only environment file):
+
+```json
+{ "version": 2, "mode": "auto",
+  "registry": { "ref": "<registry id>", "version": "<registry version>" },
+  "roles": { "builder": { "tier": "openai/economy", "settings": { "reasoning_effort": "high" } },
+             "reviewer": { "tier": "anthropic/flagship" },
+             "investigator": { "provider": "anthropic" },
+             "coordinator": { "provider": "openai", "connection": "<connection id>", "model": "<model id>" } },
+  "routes": { "escalation": { "builder": ["openai/economy", "openai/flagship"] },
+              "fallback": [{ "from": "openai/economy", "to": "anthropic/economy", "cross_provider": true,
+                             "permitted_for": "builder", "kind": "provider-outage" }] },
+  "budgets": { "spend": [{ "per": "period", "period": "P1D", "limit": "25.00", "currency": "USD" }],
+    "max_context_tokens": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "max_concurrency": null,
+    "max_retries": { "transport": 2, "repair_cycles": 1 },
+    "unknown_price": "treat-as-exhausted",
+    "on_exhaust": { "action": "route", "route": "openai/economy" } } }
+```
+
+A role names a qualified `tier`, a `provider` alone (that provider's role default), or an operator pin
+(`provider` + `connection` + `model`, recorded as a pin and never escalated automatically). A settings write refuses
+an unqualified tier or a tier the loaded registry does not bind. `routes.escalation` keys are roles or job kinds;
+entries on another provider are ignored. `routes.fallback[].kind` is `provider-outage` (the default),
+`harness-substitution`, `retired-binding` or `budget` (only authorises a budget-driven `on_exhaust` switch); `permitted_for` is a role, a job kind or `all`; a route to another provider needs
+`cross_provider: true`. `registry.version` that differs from the loaded registry gives a WARNING.
+
+Schema version 1 files (`profile`, `roles.<role>.{provider,model,effort}`) are read unchanged and converted in memory
+through the registry; `status` reports `schemaVersion: 1, migration: "available"` and a WARNING with the command.
+Nothing is rewritten until `node .mock2/bin/orchestrate.mjs migrate --scope <user|project|session> [--dry-run]`
+writes version 2 at that one scope: a version-1 role model override becomes a pinned binding at the same scope (the
+provider named at that scope, else the one registry provider whose tiers or catalog list the model, else the
+registry's `default_provider`, plus that provider's connection, and the effort named at that scope); a version-1 profile becomes role bindings to the
+profile's qualified tiers; the 1.40.0 complex-builder escalation is the registry's same-provider escalation route;
+a version-1 `fallback` (when present) becomes routes of kind `harness-substitution` that keep its conditions.
+Nothing that only the person's lower (personal) layers supplied is copied into the migrated scope: the output lists
+such items in `unset` with a WARNING, names the roles whose routes would differ for the person running it
+(`differing`), and notes that others resolve a shared file over their own settings. When the host registry cannot
+read version-1 settings (no `profiles`, or several connections), they and every route are read through the shipped
+compatibility registry with a WARNING; an error that remains names
+`--registry .mock2/standards/samples/model-registry.legacy-1.40.json`. A version-1 pin whose provider and model
+1.40.0 refused as a mismatch (for example an `openai` provider with a model listed only under `anthropic`) is now
+converted as written, since provider identity is never inferred from a name.
+`--dry-run` prints the diff, reports whether effective routes are `identical`, and writes nothing. While a scope is
+on version 1, only `mode` can be changed there. `set --profile <name>` remains for one minor release as a deprecated
+alias: it applies the registry's `profiles.<name>` as role tier bindings at that scope with a WARNING; an undefined
+name is an error. `anthropic` is the canonical provider id; `claude` is accepted only as a version-1 alias on read.
+
+## The model registry
+
+Schema `standards/schemas/model-registry.schema.json`. The registry is looked up in this order: `--registry FILE`,
+`MOCK2_MODEL_REGISTRY`, the host's `~/.mock2/model-registry.json`, then the shipped compatibility registry
+`.mock2/standards/samples/model-registry.legacy-1.40.json` (and its copy under `~/.mock2/standards/samples/`).
+
+```json
+{ "registry_version": "<semver or date stamp>", "updated_at": "<RFC 3339>", "default_provider": "<provider id>",
+  "profiles": { "<profile name>": { "<role>": "<provider>/<tier>" } },
+  "providers": { "<provider id>": {
+      "connections": [{ "id": "<connection id>", "endpoint_ref": "<opaque, no secret>", "account_ref": "<opaque>" }],
+      "tiers": { "<tier key>": { "connection": "<connection id>", "model": "<model id>", "settings_defaults": {},
+                                 "accepted_by": "<operator or policy id>", "accepted_at": "<date>" } },
+      "role_defaults": { "<role>": "<tier key>" },
+      "escalation": { "<role or job kind>": ["<tier key>", "<stronger tier key>"] },
+      "catalog": [{ "model": "<id>", "source": "api|manual", "observed_at": "<date>", "family": null,
+                    "lifecycle": "active|deprecated|retired|unknown", "shutdown_or_retire_at": null,
+                    "capabilities": { "<name>": "supported|unsupported|unknown" }, "classification": "mapped|unclassified" }],
+      "pricing": [{ "model": "<id>", "currency": "<ISO 4217>", "source": "<pricing source>", "effective_date": "<date>",
+                    "per_million": { "input": null, "cached_input_read": null, "cache_write": null, "output": null },
+                    "processing_tier": "standard" }],
+      "discovery": { "status": "ok|failed|never", "last_attempt_at": "<RFC 3339>", "last_success_at": "<RFC 3339>" } } } }
+```
+
+`standards/samples/model-registry.two-providers.sample.json` is a non-normative sample with placeholders only: for
+example, an operator might bind a provider's smallest current model to its Economy tier. That is a configurable
+binding chosen by the operator, not a rule of this standard. `standards/samples/model-registry.legacy-1.40.json` is
+the compatibility registry: the 1.40.0 profile table as data, tiers `legacy-coordinator`, `legacy-investigator`,
+`legacy-builder`, `legacy-reviewer` and `legacy-complex` per provider, `accepted_by: "mock2-1.40-defaults"`, and
+`default_provider` set to 1.40.0's default, so an installation with no settings routes exactly as 1.40.0 did. It is
+the only place the old model ids remain.
+
+## Discovery and lifecycle
+
+1. Discover through the providers' supported model-listing interfaces, after checking their current documentation
+   and the adapter or SDK version. Neither listing documents a tier, a quality class or a price: tier bindings are
+   explicit configuration, and prices come from a host-recorded pricing source with an effective date.
+2. A provider's family field is family metadata, never a quality rating; null, unknown and new values are kept, and a
+   family is never parsed from an id. Provider identity comes from the connection that listed the model.
+3. Family metadata, local tier policy, price data and task-evaluation results stay distinct. Trust, quality or
+   lowest cost is never derived from a name, a release date, a context size or a token price.
+4. A new or unmapped model is `classification: "unclassified"` and never changes an active default. It is bound only
+   by an explicit operator mapping, or by an already-configured evaluation or promotion policy naming its evidence.
+5. Each entry records provenance (`source`, `observed_at`), lifecycle and shutdown information, and capability
+   uncertainty (`unknown`).
+6. Stale or failed discovery never erases a saved binding and never triggers a fallback: the binding stays usable with
+   a WARNING naming the catalog age (older than 30 days, or the last discovery failed). A bound model reported
+   `retired`, or past its shutdown date, resolves to `binding-retired` and uses only a configured route.
+7. An attempt's identity is resolved once, at the start of the attempt, and pinned in its execution record. A
+   binding changed during the attempt does not re-resolve it; escalation or replacement is a new recorded attempt.
+
+## Escalation, fallback and budget
+
+Routes are ordered lists of qualified bindings per role or job kind; default escalation stays within the provider.
+Four mechanisms, each bounded and recorded, each preserving checkpoints:
+
+| Mechanism | Route | Attempt |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Transport retry | same binding | same attempt, up to `max_retries.transport` |
+| Capability escalation | the next stronger configured tier of the same provider | new attempt |
+| Provider-outage fallback | only a configured route permitted for the role or job kind | new attempt |
+| Human clarification | none | waits on the requester |
+
+A required capability recorded `unsupported` or `unknown` is a `capability-mismatch` listing it, unless a configured
+stronger tier later in the configured escalation route supports it. A configured route answers only its own kind of
+condition: `retired-binding` (or `provider-outage`) a retired model, `provider-outage` an outage or an unavailable
+model, `harness-substitution` a native harness that cannot run the provider. Cross-provider fallback requires
+`cross_provider: true` and a `permitted_for` match; the result is `planned-fallback` with both routes, why, the old and
+new provider and model, and whether settings changed (`fallbacks[]` keeps every step). A replacement route goes
+through the same checks as the original (capability, retirement, native harness, outage, budget, effort and price
+class); one that fails ends in that status, never `planned-fallback`. Without such a route an outage leaves the job
+`waiting`. No provider switch is ever silent.
+
+Repair escalation: one repair cycle runs on the same route. When its recheck still fails, a further equivalent
+repair is refused and the configured escalation runs as a new attempt; with no route configured the job waits
+(`waiting_reason: failed-attempt`). Unresolved scope, a capability limit or an exhausted budget escalate earlier.
+
+Budgets: a null limit is not configured, never 0. Spend is checked against execution-record estimates, each record
+counted once by `accounting_owner`. An exhausted limit (spend, context, output, concurrency, retries), or an unknown
+price where a spend limit needs one under `unknown_price: treat-as-exhausted` (the default), takes exactly
+`on_exhaust`: `wait` (status `waiting`, `waiting_reason: capacity`), `ask` (status `waiting`,
+`waiting_reason: requester`, and a `needs_decision` record of kind `proposed-decision`) or `route` (the named binding,
+`resolution_source: budget-on-exhaust`, recorded in `fallbacks[]` and checked again for capability, retirement,
+harness, effort and price class). A `route` is used only when the target relieves every exhausted limit: spend at
+the target's price, and an output limit only when the target's own `max_output_tokens` brings the job under it. No
+route relieves a context limit (the job's own size), a concurrency limit or a retry limit: a concurrency limit always
+waits (`waiting_reason: capacity`) and a retry limit always waits (`waiting_reason: failed-attempt`), naming the limit,
+whatever `on_exhaust` says; with `ask` the `needs_decision` record is still added. `on_exhaust` is applied at most
+once: every route reached after it (its target, and any fallback from the target) is evaluated again against every
+limit, and one still over a limit waits with the reason, without applying `on_exhaust` again. An `on_exhaust.route`
+that names the exhausted route itself waits. An `on_exhaust.route` on another provider is used only when a configured
+cross-provider route of kind `budget` or `provider-outage` from the current tier to it is permitted for the role or job
+kind (a `harness-substitution` or `retired-binding` route never authorises it); the result is then `planned-fallback`,
+otherwise the job waits. A more expensive binding no route names is never chosen. A limit with no
+`on_exhaust` waits with a visible reason and `doctor` prints a WARNING; it never adds an approval prompt. Under
+`unknown_price: allow` work proceeds and spend reads `unknown (n unpriced calls)`. Spend so far comes from
+`resolve --records DIR` (execution records: per job by `job_id`, per work item by `work_item_id`, per period by
+`started_at` within the last `P<n>D|W|M`, a month counted as 30 days) or from the job's `budget_usage`
+(`{ spend: [{ per, period, currency, amount, unpriced_calls }], concurrency, retries: { transport, repair_cycles } }`).
+A period or work-item limit with no usage for it, a job with no token estimate, an estimate amount that names no
+currency, and a price or record in another currency than the limit's are unknown, and follow `unknown_price`;
+currencies are never converted. A per-job limit with no usage for it counts the job's earlier attempts as 0 spent:
+supply `--records` or `budget_usage` to count them. The host's numeric price class
+(`max_price_class`, `model_price_classes` in a capability file) is a restriction signal only, never a model tier.
+
+## Attribution: the execution record
+
+Schema `standards/schemas/execution-record.v1.schema.json`, `schema: "mock2.execution/1"`, one record per model-call
+group within an attempt or per call (`granularity` says which). Unknown values are null.
+
+- Work identity: `work_item_id`, `plan_revision_id`, `job_id`, `attempt_id`, `parent_task_id`, `child_task_ids`,
+  `environment`, `actor`, `role`, `started_at`, `ended_at`, `event_ids`, `trace_id`.
+- Route: `requested.{provider, tier, model}`, `connection_ref`, `registry_version`, `policy_version`,
+  `resolution_source`.
+- Identity: `observed_model` and `identity_evidence`, one of `observed` (the provider's response), `host-reported`,
+  `configured` (nothing reported; the route was configured), `unavailable` and `inconsistent` (every value kept in
+  `inconsistent_values`). An unknown observed identity is never filled with the requested model.
+- Settings: `requested_settings` and `effective_settings` (effort, processing or service tier, speed, output limit)
+  as reported; unknown stays unknown.
+- Usage: `provider_request_ids`, `provider_response_ids`, `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cache_read_tokens`,
+  `cache_write_tokens`, `reasoning_tokens`, `chargeable_extras`. `input_tokens` is uncached input only. A response
+  whose input count includes cached reads (reported apart as cached tokens) is mapped by subtracting them; one whose
+  input count already excludes cache reads and writes is used as reported (`usageFromResponse` in the library does
+  both). Reasoning tokens are counted within output tokens.
+- Cost: `estimate { amount, currency, pricing_source, pricing_effective_date, assumptions[] }` and, separately,
+  `billed { amount, currency, source, reconciled_at }`. Missing pricing or an unknown input or output count is null,
+  never zero; amounts keep full precision. A null processing tier is priced at `standard`, and a provider's
+  `default` processing tier is the same as `standard`. `accounting_owner` is the `record_id` that owns the cost; a
+  coordinator's view of a child's usage names the child and is not counted again; a record whose owner is not in the
+  set is counted.
+- Workflow links: `escalations[]`, `retries[]`, `fallbacks[]`, `review_finding_ids`, `candidate_id`,
+  `authorization_ref`, `check_run_ids`, `deployment_record_id`, `recovery_record_id`, `limitations[]`.
+
+Provider metadata is attribution evidence, not cryptographic proof of execution. A review is independent when its
+actor differs from the builder's; the same provider or family is allowed, the same actor is not. The run ledger keeps
+its `model` column; an optional `route` column after `weakened` reads
+`<provider>/<tier> → <model or unknown> (<evidence level>)`, with `; n delegated: …` for delegated work, and the
+handoff's `Surface / Model` line carries the same route text. Earlier rows are not back-filled.
+
+## Comparative cost
+
+`node .mock2/bin/orchestrate.mjs cost-compare --records <dir> [--registry FILE]` reads execution records (`*.json`, a
+record or a list) and writes nothing. Per provider, each with its own bindings: the actual configured-routing
+estimate, `economy_only` and `flagship_only`. A provider with no such tier reads `n/a (no such tier)`; no substitute
+is used. The counterfactuals are labelled "same-usage counterfactual — not a measured rerun" and list cache
+assumptions, the processing tier used, the price source and effective date, and excluded costs (billed amounts,
+chargeable extras). Totals are kept per currency (`by_currency`; `total` is a number only when there is one currency,
+otherwise `several currencies`). An unknown price or token count makes that provider's total `unknown` with its count
+of unpriced calls. No claim
+is made that economy-only would complete the work or that mixed routing equals flagship quality; completion,
+validation, rework and escalation counts are reported separately. Currency totals may span providers. The report is
+information: no target, nothing per person, never a gate (PRO §U).
+
+## Documented and implemented
+
+| Capability | Here | The orchestration tool |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Qualified tiers, registry, precedence, statuses | contract | implemented and tested |
+| Version-1 read, migrate, `--profile` alias | contract | implemented and tested |
+| Discovery merge, unclassified models, stale catalog warning | contract | `mergeDiscovery` implemented and tested; listing calls are the host's |
+| Escalation, fallback, budgets, repair escalation | contract | implemented and tested |
+| Pinned attempts and the execution record | contract | `pinAttempt`, `buildExecutionRecord` implemented and tested; writing records is the host's |
+| Identity evidence | contract | `classifyIdentity` implemented and tested; observation is the host's |
+| Comparative cost | contract | `cost-compare` implemented and tested |
+| Provider connections, model calls, price sources | the host's | not implemented here |
