index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4d8c01b1a1d112001edb393dc136ca710d8c81d7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/core/cpr/Work-Lifecycle.md
@@ -0,0 +1,127 @@
+# Work lifecycle: from idea to release, as events
+
+Contract and guidance (1.43.0). One idea-to-release workflow that a person without a technical background can follow
+and an integrating tool's backend can implement and draw. It maps onto Relay events first and adds three optional
+Relay observations only where nothing fits. Nothing here is a build gate and nothing invents an approval: plan
+approval is never deployment permission, and releases use the release authorization that already exists (PRO §P).
+
+## Stages
+
+| Stage key | Stage | What happens |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `problem` | Problem or opportunity | Someone states what is wrong or wanted, in their words |
+| `idea` | Idea | A proposal, with the AI's interpretation kept separate from the request |
+| `investigation` | Investigation and discussion | Questions, answers, feedback, risks, acceptance checks, plan revisions |
+| `authorization` | Approved plan and implementation authorization | A decision bound to one plan revision |
+| `queue` | Queue | Authorized ready work waits with its plan, priority, dependencies, resources and acceptance |
+| `build` | Build | Bounded independent tasks under the existing isolation, concurrency, budget, module and worktree rules |
+| `validate` | Validate and review | A separate reviewer; checks bound to the exact candidate; a bounded repair loop |
+| `deploy` | Deploy | The exact checked candidate, released under existing authorization, verified live |
+| `observe` | Observe and learn | Findings feed back as new ideas |
+
+States: `active`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `failed`, `cancelled`, `interrupted`, `recovering`, `done`. A waiting state
+names one reason: `capacity`, `requester`, `blocker` or `failed-attempt`. This queue is the work queue; it is
+distinct from the release queue of PRO §M.10.
+
+## Rules
+
+1. Records stay distinguishable and carry provenance: the request, the AI's interpretation, questions, feedback,
+   proposed and approved decisions, acceptance checks, risks and plan revisions, each with author, time and revision.
+2. Investigation is not authorization.
+3. Authorization is bound to the plan revision it covers. A job on that revision dispatches with no new request; an
+   existing authorization is reused, never asked for again. A later revision recorded as non-material reuses it. A
+   material change of scope is recorded as needing a new decision (`record.added`, kind `proposed-decision`,
+   referencing both revisions) and the job waits with `waiting_reason: requester`
+   (`authorizationDecision` in the orchestration library).
+4. The queue holds authorized ready work only.
+5. Build tasks are bounded and independent; background agents start none of their own.
+6. Review runs in a separate context by a different actor; the same model family is allowed, and the builder is never
+   its own reviewer. Checks record `passed`, `failed`, `skipped`, `missing`, `stale` or `inconclusive`, bound to the
+   exact candidate.
+7. Repair is bounded: one cycle on the same route; when its recheck still fails, the configured escalation runs as a
+   new attempt before any equivalent repair (`Model-Routing.md`, repair escalation).
+8. Deploy uses the existing release authorization. A plan approval is never presented as deployment permission.
+9. The exact checked candidate is released with its release record, rollback or recovery, and live verification;
+   observation follows and its findings feed back.
+10. Cancelled, interrupted, recovering, blocked and failed work stays visible. Completion is never fabricated.
+
+## Event contract
+
+Relay events carry the lifecycle first:
+
+| Need | Relay event |
+| --- | --- |
+| Work identity, stage label, checkpoint, next step, needs input | `task.created`, `task.updated` |
+| Attempts | `run.started`, `run.finished` |
+| Parallel tasks, repair loops | `step.started`, `step.activity`, `step.finished` (one attempt number per try) |
+| Check results on the candidate | `check.recorded` (schema 2) |
+| Release | `deployment.recorded` |
+| Repository history | `history.commit.observed` |
+
+Three observations are new, because nothing above fits. They are schema version 3 with minimum reader Relay 1.3.0,
+and the repository producer writes them only when `.mock2/checks.json` sets `"relay": {"lifecycleEvidence": true}`,
+off by default exactly like `relay.checkEvidence`, and to be turned on only after every host the repository delivers
+to reads Relay 1.3.0. `node .mock2/bin/relay.mjs lifecycle` reads `{ "type", "taskId", "data" }` on standard input;
+while the setting is off it reports `{"emitted": false, "reason": …}` and writes nothing. `MOCK2_RELAY=off` records
+nothing; `MOCK2_RELAY_LIFECYCLE_EVIDENCE=1|0` overrides the setting for one process. The work item id is the Relay
+`taskId`.
+
+| Type | Data |
+| --- | --- |
+| `record.added` | `recordId`, `kind` (`request`, `interpretation`, `question`, `answer`, `feedback`, `proposed-decision`, `approved-decision`, `acceptance-check`, `risk`, `plan-revision`, `authorization`, `finding`, `observation`), `author`, `summary`; optional `refs` (record ids), `provenance`, `text`, `revision`, `material` (a plan revision's materiality), `attribution` |
+| `work.state.changed` | `stage` (the nine keys above), `state`, `waitingReason` (exactly when `state` is `waiting`); optional `summary`, `label`, `attribution`, `revision` |
+| `execution.recorded` | `recordId` and the execution record (`record`, `mock2.execution/1`) or its SHA-256 `digest`; optional `attribution`; may carry `runId` |
+
+A lifecycle stage is display context: it never marks a task complete or deployed, and an execution record is
+attribution evidence, not proof that a model ran. Version-1 and version-2 history reads unchanged.
+
+## Storytelling
+
+A story follows one work item id through every stage as timestamped, ordered events: the source time (`at`) gives the
+chronology, the host's sequence gives replay. Each plain-language label carries the role and the qualified tier with
+its identity evidence, for example `builder · openai/economy · observed`. A story is `source: recorded` (from a
+stream) or `source: sample` (illustrative). A 24-hour view is a reporting window, not a promise of duration.
+
+The reference story (Guidance, illustrative) is `standards/samples/story-track-support-request.sample.json`, with
+placeholder providers `<provider-a>` and `<provider-b>`.
+
+### "Track my support request" in one page
+
+**08:00 — the problem.** A requester writes: customers want to see the status of their support request without
+calling. The work item opens and its stage reads *Problem*.
+
+**08:20 — the idea.** The investigator (on `<provider-a>/balanced`) restates it as a status page per request, updated
+when an agent changes the request. The request and the interpretation stay two separate records, so anyone can see
+what was asked and what the AI understood.
+
+**09:00–11:30 — the conversation.** The investigator asks whether customers should see internal notes; the requester
+answers no. A first plan adds a page and notifications; the requester says notifications are not wanted yet, so a
+second plan drops them. An acceptance check is written down: a customer sees the current status of their own request
+and no one else's. A risk is noted: request ids in links could expose another customer's request. A third plan adds
+the ownership check.
+
+**11:45 — approval.** The requester approves plan revision 3 and authorizes implementation of revision 3 only. If the
+plan later changed materially, the work would stop and ask again; small recorded corrections reuse this approval.
+
+**12:00–13:10 — the queue.** The authorized work waits because build capacity is busy. The story says why it waits.
+
+**13:10–16:00 — the build.** Three tasks run at the same time: the status data and ownership check and the status page
+on `<provider-a>/economy`, the change-history feed on `<provider-a>/balanced`. Each task's model is recorded with how
+it is known (here: observed in the provider's response).
+
+**16:20 — the review.** A different reviewer, on `<provider-b>/flagship`, finds one issue: a signed-in customer can
+open a request they do not own. The reviewer is not any of the builders.
+
+**16:30–17:40 — the repair.** One repair runs on the same route and the recheck still fails. Instead of trying the same
+thing again, the work escalates within the same provider to `<provider-a>/flagship` as a new attempt, and the recheck
+passes. Both attempts stay in the story.
+
+**18:30 — final checks.** Tests, types and the browser check pass on the exact candidate that will be released.
+
+**20:00 — the release.** The release helper releases that candidate under the release authorization the project
+already had, and live verification confirms a customer sees only their own request.
+
+**Overnight — observation.** The feature runs while people use it.
+
+**07:30 next day — learning.** Customers ask to be told when the status changes. That finding becomes a new request,
+and a new story begins.
