+## AA. Provider-scoped model tiers, workflow and attribution
+
+**Status:** published 2026-10-30 in standards 1.43.0. **Applies:** every repository whose agents the
+orchestration tool routes, and every integrating tool's backend that dispatches or reports agent work
+[general]. **Extends:** §F, §I, §M.11, §M.12, §P and §U; an application's own AI stays under §R.
+
+Mock2 defines the policy, the data contracts and the acceptance; each integrating tool's backend
+supplies provider connections, available models, tier bindings, execution adapters and controls. The
+contracts are `Model-Routing.md` (tiers, registry, settings, escalation, budgets, the execution record,
+comparative cost) and `Work-Lifecycle.md` (stages, rules, events, storytelling), installed in
+`.mock2/standards/` with their schemas in `standards/schemas/` and non-normative samples in
+`standards/samples/`. Levels: a Rule is a review finding, not a stop; a Contract is a schema or event
+an integrating tool implements, and one that does not says so; Tool behaviour is the orchestration
+tool refusing its own action with a visible status; a WARNING never changes an exit status; Guidance
+explains. Nothing here is a build gate (§B).
+
+### AA.1 Tiers are scoped to a provider
+
+1. A tier is a qualified key `provider_id/tier_key`: `openai/economy` and `anthropic/economy` are
+   distinct and bound independently. An unqualified key never dispatches (`unqualified-tier`).
+2. The starting labels are `economy`, `balanced` and `flagship`. Each provider keeps its own mapping
+   and tier set, may omit a label or add a provider-scoped tier (`anthropic/long-context`), and no
+   ranking across providers is implied.
+3. A binding is configuration, never a family-to-tier rule. Concrete model ids live only in a
+   versioned model registry the host owns; standard and rule text name no current model release, and
+   samples use placeholders. Adopting a new model is a registry change.
+4. A binding is connection-scoped (an account, workspace or endpoint identity, never a secret) and
+   never selects through another connection or provider.
+5. Roles: `coordinator`, `investigator`, `builder`, `reviewer` and `release-helper` (§P's runner, with
+   no promotion authority). Effort, output limit, processing speed and required capabilities are
+   settings independent of the tier. Precedence, highest first: an authorized job override (per
+   invocation, or an explicit chat request at session scope), the environment's role configuration,
+   project, user, then the provider's role default in the registry; the registry's `default_provider`
+   applies when no layer names a provider, and without one every role is `binding-missing`. Personal
+   off outranks all of them for whether to dispatch. Resolution ends in an explicit route or a visible
+   status naming what is missing; there is no silent default.
+6. Every execution records the registry and policy version and the binding it used.
+
+### AA.2 Discovery and lifecycle
+
+Models are discovered through the providers' supported listing interfaces, after checking their
+current documentation and the adapter or SDK version. A provider's family field is family metadata,
+not a quality rating, and is never parsed from an id; family metadata, local tier policy, price data
+and task-evaluation results stay distinct. A new or unmapped model is `unclassified` and never changes
+an active default; it is bound only by an explicit operator mapping or an already-configured
+evaluation or promotion policy that names its evidence. Stale or failed discovery never erases a saved
+binding and never triggers a fallback: the binding stays usable with a WARNING naming the catalog age.
+A retired bound model is `binding-retired` and uses only a configured route. An attempt's route is
+resolved once, when it starts, and pinned in its execution record; a binding changed during the
+attempt does not re-resolve it.
+
+### AA.3 Escalation, fallback and budget
+
+Model choice: an inexpensive capable tier for bounded, well-specified work; a stronger configured
+tier for uncertainty, difficult design, complex investigation, demanding review or a failed repair
+cycle. Neither the cheapest tier for every task nor a premium tier for every stage. Routes are ordered
+lists of qualified bindings per role or job kind, and default escalation stays within the provider.
+Transport retry (same binding, same attempt), capability escalation (a stronger configured tier, a new
+attempt), provider-outage fallback (only when configured and permitted) and human clarification
+(waits on the requester) are distinct, bounded and recorded, and each preserves checkpoints. A route to
+another provider needs `cross_provider: true` and a `permitted_for` scope; a same-named tier elsewhere
+never authorises a switch, and the record states why, the old and new provider and model, and what
+changed. After one repair cycle whose recheck still fails, the configured escalation runs as a new
+attempt before any equivalent repair; unresolved scope, a capability limit or an exhausted budget
+escalate earlier. An exhausted budget, or an unknown price under `unknown_price: treat-as-exhausted`,
+takes exactly the configured `on_exhaust` (`wait`, `ask` or a named `route`); a more expensive binding
+no route names is never chosen, and a limit with no `on_exhaust` waits with a visible reason and a
+`doctor` WARNING. No provider switch is ever silent.
+
+### AA.4 Attribution
+
+An execution record (`standards/schemas/execution-record.v1.schema.json`) keeps the requested route,
+the configured files and the observed identity as separate facts. `identity_evidence` is `observed`
+(the provider's response), `host-reported`, `configured` (nothing reported), `unavailable` or
+`inconsistent` (both values kept). An unknown observed identity is never filled with the requested
+model and called verified; provider metadata is attribution evidence, not cryptographic proof. Usage,
+an estimate and a separately billed amount are recorded; missing pricing is unknown, never zero, and
+`accounting_owner` counts each cost once. A review is independent when its actor differs from the
+builder's; the same provider or family is allowed. The ledger and the handoff carry the route as text
+(§I).
+
+### AA.5 Lifecycle and events
+
+Work moves through nine stages: problem, idea, investigation, authorization, queue, build, validate,
+deploy, observe. Investigation is not authorization. An authorization is bound to the plan revision it
+covers and reused for it, never asked for again; a material revision is recorded as needing a new
+decision, and the job waits on the requester. A plan approval is never deployment permission: deploys
+use the release authorization that already exists (§K, §P). Cancelled, interrupted, recovering,
+blocked and failed work stays visible, and completion is never fabricated. Relay events carry the
+lifecycle first; three schema-3 observations (`record.added`, `work.state.changed`,
+`execution.recorded`) ship off by default behind `relay.lifecycleEvidence`, turned on only after every
+host the repository delivers to reads Relay 1.3.0.
+
+### AA.6 Comparative cost
+
+`orchestrate.mjs cost-compare` reads execution records and writes nothing. Per provider, with its own
+bindings, it reports the actual routing estimate and the economy-only and flagship-only same-usage
+counterfactuals, labelled as not a measured rerun; a missing tier reads `n/a (no such tier)` and an
+unknown price makes that provider's total `unknown` with its count of unpriced calls. It is
+information, as §U is.
+
+### AA.7 Storytelling
+
+A story follows one work item id through every stage as ordered, timestamped events, each label
+carrying the role, the qualified tier and the identity evidence (`builder · openai/economy ·
+observed`). The reference story "Track my support request" is Guidance and illustrative; a 24-hour
+view is a reporting window, not a promise.
+
+### AA.8 Documented and implemented
+
+The orchestration tool implements qualified tiers, the registry lookup, precedence and statuses,
+reading version-1 settings, `migrate` and the deprecated `--profile` alias, the discovery merge,
+escalation, fallback, budgets and repair escalation, pinned attempts, the execution record's builder,
+identity classification and `cost-compare`, each tested with fake catalogs and no paid call. Provider
+listing calls, model calls, writing execution records, price sources, billing reconciliation and the
+viewer of lifecycle events are the host's. A host that does not implement a part says so, and a
+documented contract is never reported as a running implementation.
