+Accept ordinary chat requests to change mode, role tier bindings, settings and scope. Translate them
+into the tool's set/reset commands; use session scope unless a persistent scope was requested. A
+model id is a registry edit through the host, never a settings file. Read back the resolved routes
+and apply or plan the native adapter as the guide describes. Do not invent a provider switch,
+persistence scope or successful host action: another provider is used only through a configured
+route permitted for that role, and the switch is recorded. Re-resolve before each dispatch; an
+attempt keeps the route it started with. Use an inexpensive capable tier for bounded work and the
+configured stronger tier for uncertainty, demanding review or a failed repair cycle. An off request
+also accounts for active workers through the harness. A route is a request, not evidence of
+execution: record the requested route, the configured files and the observed model separately, with
+the identity evidence level (`observed`, `host-reported`, `configured`, `unavailable`,
+`inconsistent`). No permission, account limit, budget or price-class restriction is bypassed. An
+unavailable route does not stop independent implementation or testing. Review stays independent (a
+different actor; the same provider is allowed) and unavailable review stays unverified (PRO current
+§M.12, §AA).
