-  "changelog": "1.42.0: Lessons from one application's release train. Final batteries: battery.mjs --seed-cache-from worktree|queue carries fingerprint-verified reuse into the clone, a build declares an identity so a reused consumer needs no build (recorded not applicable, neither pass nor missing), runner-owned variables leave the environment fingerprint, battery.cleanEnv gives the clone a clean environment, and a passed battery writes its cache back. check.mjs notices a changed candidate after each heavy check (--no-early-stop keeps the old report), names a file that dominates its check three runs running for repair, and warns on a repeat failure with no known-failures entry. Briefs: the Checks: line defaults to no full run before review, and doctor --brief lints briefs. Release tools: gates.mjs runs a release prompt's gates, keeps saved values and refuses placeholders; records.mjs makes the records commit from an exact list and lists other sessions' records for step 0; queue.mjs keeps the release queue (base, withdraw, absorb, slots); port.mjs fails closed; a release-prompt template filled by gen.mjs release-prompt; generator templates for a statement-parsing migration guard, a host-side read-only query helper and a shared non-app path list. worktree-lifecycle archive-evidence copies evidence and verifies it before closeout. PRO text: environment-mode parity and service-worker route parity (E), real-path smoke of new cross-site flows (N.7), whole-prompt templates and derived markers (P.1, P.6), gates (P.2, P.3), a promoted-during-release branch (P.8), the out-of-order routine (M.10), other sessions' records in step 0 (N.8), the migration manifest and parsing guard (N.11), the read-only production helper and announced audit-writing requests (N.5, N.11), the rollback-slot decision (K.3), the ratchet seed for new states of existing pages (K.6), and standing instructions live in the repository. No new gate. No project's own settings change. 1.40.0: Downloadable offline source ZIP, exact source identity, SHA-256 checksums and transfer instructions for local-folder upgrades, VS Code and Git rebasing. 1.39.0: The application foundation stack. Every new PRO application is provisioned by its host with PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector, S3-compatible object storage (SeaweedFS by default; any S3-compatible provider such as Cloudflare R2 instead) and a local embedding service (development quality, with a warning to switch to OpenAI or another provider), as one Compose unit in its own deployment. The database is reached over a local socket with peer authentication; its only network listener, on loopback, admits just the read-only reporting role, with SCRAM. Storage and embeddings switch off per deployment and no switch deletes data; bundled storage that holds objects stays on until a person picks a replacement and either copies everything, verified, or deliberately starts fresh. An embedding model or dimension change re-embeds into a new validated index; vector spaces are never mixed and failover never crosses them. Feature code reaches objects, embeddings and vector search only through the host's adapters. Secrets are held encrypted by the host, never in env files. Readiness is reported only after a real operation, and mock and local providers say what they are. SQLite and other engines are refused for any server role unless the developer explicitly asked and a person recorded the request on the project; DATABASE_URL is required and a missing database fails start-up, never a fallback. A new application records Profile: cpr; none is for existing repositories. pgvector is required for new applications and the stack's images are pinned. Existing applications are not changed; moving one onto the stack is a migration a person confirms, from a restorable snapshot, with the database verified table by table, rollback on failure or on request, and nothing deleted (PRO current \u00a7W.6; \u00a7D, \u00a7J.4, \u00a7J.9, \u00a7Q, \u00a7R, \u00a7W.3; Addendum A.2, A.8). 1.38.0: Numeric targets are planned before code. A Done-when item that carries a number (latency, size, throughput, memory, time, count) gets a line under Budget estimates in the Authorized scope: the mechanism expected to meet it and the rough arithmetic, rate \u00d7 cost per unit against the budget, with what grows over the longest expected session. An estimate that misses is resolved in the design before code, or marked [OPEN] with the design assumed until the first measurement settles it. With no numeric target nothing is added, so a small change keeps three lines; a small change with a number adds the estimate as a fourth. Each failure case states the rule the design keeps. The measurement runs against the first working skeleton, runs that prove scale, latency and durability come before feature and visual runs, and a result reads measured vs target with PASS or FAIL. Work on a shared component starts from its docs and recorded decisions, and what a cycle learns goes back into them (in scope when the cycle touches those docs, otherwise a follow-up). Review checks the estimates and results; a missing estimate is a low follow-up, never back-filled. Text only; none of it holds up building, testing or committing (capacity \u00a75, \u00a710, \u00a713; PRO current \u00a7E). 1.37.0: Internal modules and guided reuse. Setup infers boundaries and records reuse intent while preserving explicit decisions. Compiler-backed checks report private imports, undeclared dependencies, cycles and unverified coverage. Read-only source-folder plans expose compatibility, host bindings, namespace conflicts, migrations and exact changes; selected application preserves local source and catalog customizations. Real receiving-project interaction evidence is separate from declarations and fake-host checks. Existing applications are never automatically converted or transferred. 1.36.0: Task worktree lifecycle. Durable ownership and receipts in the common Git directory support read-only inventory, bounded closeout plans and interruption recovery. Integration or self-contained preservation, protected-content checks, exact worktree identity and current host inactivity/admission evidence guard selected closeout. Checkout, branch and registration cleanup remain separate; existing backlog is not automatically removed. Host process management and scheduling remain explicit adapter responsibilities. 1.35.0: Portable Git readiness. Read-only host and project observations distinguish an absent tool, an unversioned folder, an empty initialization and a committed baseline. Authorized preparation uses a reviewed bounded selection, preserving existing history and user edits. An optional remote keeps that local history and uses explicit safe synchronization with truthful offline, conflict and uncertain states. Existing first-time setup and build preparation reuse the readiness result; host installation, credentials, scheduling and durable execution remain adapter responsibilities. 1.34.0: Integrated application first-time setup. Existing init and resumable setup collect purpose, audience, access, data, experience and first-version scope; a project-owned blueprint keeps research provenance, assumptions and conditional module choices. A composable base application generator provides the selected foundation with secure instance owner setup and server permissions. Adoption and standards upgrades preserve application source and answers. Generated source, configured dependencies and demonstrated live behavior remain distinct. 1.33.0: Configurable agent routing. A deterministic tool resolves default-on auto mode, provider profiles and per-role model/effort overrides across personal, project and chat scope; direct bypass and off stop new dispatches, with personal off retained until explicitly reenabled. Native adapters plan and apply owned role definitions while preserving existing settings, report unsupported routes and required host actions, and distinguish requested from actual models. Chat requests use the same settings tool. No persistent service or external provider runner is implied. 1.32.1: Names out of the public text. Mock2's public text names concepts and abilities only: the names of other products, tools used as idea sources, applications, customers, people, companies and hosts, and one deployment's paths, job ids and private commits, are replaced by what they stood for (\"one hosted application\", \"the hosting product\", \"the build environment\", \"an AI IDE's requirement phrasing\"). That covers the PRO v1.1 text and its Addendum A, kept as published except that names of other products, applications, customers and people, and the file names and identifiers that pointed to them, are generalised, with their figures kept; PRO current; the run-ledger rule, the define prompt and the template's production checklist; the Relay example, whose comment author is now a role, and its overview image, re-rendered from the example; the public demo, rebuilt, with six old build files removed; the site, the generator's defaults and mock2-core's own records, where the published run ledger's model cells read \"not published\" and three review screenshots blur their small thumbnail of the earlier image. No rule changes meaning, and projects receive only corrected text and images. A repository seeded earlier keeps its own state/production-checklist.md and may delete the old example comment in it. mock2-core gains its own names check, public-names.test.mjs, run in its suite and before every publication: names read with words split at case and digit changes, across line breaks, spaced and joined, and in file paths, against a published hashed list that holds only names an earlier release already published (the private list never leaves the build environment); shape rules for deployment and home paths, private addresses, job ids, emails, model ids, session links and ticket ids; one list of allowed hosts, with or without a scheme; and a list of reviewed images. Nothing new ships to project repositories as a check, and 000-READ-FIRST.md states the rule for authors. Relay stays 1.2.1; only its example data and example image changed. 1.32.0: Storage, retention and cleanup. A new rule for all work, mock2-storage.md, and PRO current \u00a7V say that nothing is removed without a retention setting that is on or a cleanup a person selected, and that storage growth is information, never a gate. A storage check is read-only, runs nothing from the application and reads a repository only as an unprivileged user; it shows an application's storage by category, logical beside allocated size and the space snapshots hold, counting a shared file once and naming what it could not measure. No cleanup removes the running application, data, secrets, records, a shared backing directory or a protected release (the live, the previous and every candidate); nothing is eligible only because it is large or old, and an age that could not be measured makes nothing eligible; and neither a cleanup nor the sealing of a release follows a link, even one swapped in during the run. A release cap counts the live release and the previous one, taken from the deployment history, never a candidate, and removes the oldest releases outside the protected set first (\u00a7K.2). A cleanup is a person's selection (none, one, several, categories or all eligible; nothing preselected; snapshots only by a separate, explicit choice; one approval), rechecked before each deletion, rescanned afterwards and audited. Retention settings per application come with documented defaults (2 releases counting the live and the previous one, 3 snapshots, caches and test artifacts 14 days, temporary files 2 days, rotated logs 14 days and, on the application's own machine, a 256 MB journal) that the host turns on for a new application it creates empty and proposes, with their effect first, for an existing, imported, cloned, restored or migrated one; log retention removes rotated logs only, and a kept database dump keeps the files that restore it. Storage alerts notify and never block; the PRO rule, the production checklist and capacity \u00a77, where an agent removes the clones, runtimes and worktrees it created, carry the short forms. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.31.0: An update by hand on a Linux server, without PowerShell. repo-init.sh --check prints the repository's release, the published one and what an update would add, update, keep and leave unchanged, and changes nothing, so a server may run it on a schedule. repo-init.sh --update downloads the release's own upgrader with every module it imports, checks each against release.json, and lands the release on a new branch mock2/standards-<version> as one commit of exactly the paths it wrote, with installed.json and the executable bits; each Mock2 file the repository adjusted is kept with the release's version beside it as <name>.mock2-new, which is never committed, and nothing is pushed. It never writes through a symbolic link or commits a path that held the developer's own work, makes the branch before it applies, and checks git's identity first. The session-start line that refuses a conflict now ends with that command, doctor lists the .mock2-new files, and the entry block tells every agent to offer the command and how to merge each pair on the update's branch: start from the release's version and re-apply the adjustment. PRO current \u00a7M.9 is amended for it. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.30.0: Background agents. The agent the developer works with, the coordinating agent, may hand independent leaf items of a SPLIT plan that change disjoint files to background agents (a subagent, a background task, a cloud task, a new session), each from a brief in files: the item's context block with Agent:, Owns:, Shared:, Checks: and Returns: lines, and a file the block names when more is needed (a release prompt, the review handoff, or state/briefs/<item id>.md). Each works in its own worktree and branch, commits only the files it owns, starts no background agent of its own, and returns its result as its own section of state/handoff.md, its branch's last commit, which the coordinating agent reads with git show. The coordinating agent alone edits the shared records, integrates in plan order, runs the checks on the integrated tree and cites that run, keeps review in a context that built none of it and leaves promotion to the developer; a background agent that runs a release prompt is its runner and writes that prompt's records, and a surface that cannot start background agents runs the same briefs in turn. Nothing here gates building or testing (.mock2/capacity.md \u00a716, PRO current \u00a7M.11). Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.29.0: A broken release no longer reaches every repository unseen. The standards' publisher smoke-tests each release with mock2-core's release tools, scripts/release-smoke.sh (Linux and macOS) and scripts/release-smoke.ps1 (Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7), which install it from a clean home folder into fresh repositories, against the candidate served locally before it is published and against the live site after. They check the machine install; repo-init.sh in a new repository (on Windows, a repository seeded from the installed template); the path an existing repository takes, a repository seeded with the release before by that release's own repo-init.sh (on Windows, its installed template), given a project commit and then upgraded by its own session-start upgrade (upgrade.mjs --from-site --if-clean --commit); and a commit through the git hooks when the release ships them, which must leave the hooks' own record. Every file is compared with release.json byte for byte, and each check prints PASS or FAIL with counts and the first differing paths. The tools are never shipped to a project repository, and a failure is a checklist item on the release, never a gate. PRO current \u00a7M.9 says a standards release is smoke-tested this way, with a table of the environments tested, supported but not run, or unverified. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.28.0: Checks run on every commit and push without anyone remembering to run them. The template's .githooks/ runs the quick checks (check.mjs --fast) before each commit and the full checks before each push. A hook never blocks and never waits: a check that needs the shared host budget while it is held or busy is reported missing (check.mjs gains MOCK2_BUDGET_WAIT=0), and a run still going after 60 s before a commit or 600 s before a push is stopped and recorded as not finished. Each hook prints at most three lines and records the run with the commit or the push in .mock2/evidence/hooks/, a folder that ignores itself; a pre-commit record says when the work tree held changes the commit leaves out. Skipping is allowed (--no-verify, MOCK2_HOOKS=off), and a commit made with no result is recorded as skipped at the next hook run; nothing runs during a rebase, cherry-pick, merge, revert or bisect, and no hook writes a tracked file. Init and repo-init.sh set core.hooksPath to .githooks, and in an existing repository the session start does (the one after the upgrade that brings the hooks, and the first in a fresh clone), only when Git has no other hooks path and the repository no hooks of its own; otherwise they set nothing and say how to opt in, and core.hooksPath .git/hooks or MOCK2_HOOKS=off keep the hooks off. The template records the hooks executable, and an upgrade with --commit records the bit with core.fileMode=false too, and the session start records it for the release's executable files an earlier commit left without it. The post-commit hook also refreshes the repository map, so refresh.mjs --install-hook adds no second set of hooks, and doctor reports the hooks' state and the last result. Not a gate; Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.27.0: Each release's turns can be counted. The run ledger gains six optional columns after release: nudges (the developer's messages that only kept the task going, each counted once), item, feature, accepted, regressions and weakened; an older ledger stays valid, earlier rows are never back-filled, and a value nothing observed is unknown, never 0, so accepted, regressions and weakened record unknown until later work feeds them. STOPPED SHORT stays the result value and is counted there. The new report tool node .mock2/bin/measures.mjs reads every ledger table by column name and the change records, prints per version the turns, minutes, STOPPED SHORT turns, nudges, tool calls, checks, deploys, accepted, regressions, weakened, runs per feature and change records with the number of unknown rows in each total, and names rows its headers cannot read; --out replaces only a summary it wrote. The counts describe different work per version and are not a speed claim; they carry no target, threshold or per-person view, and the pooled delivery figures stay the host's (PRO current \u00a7I and \u00a7U, amended). 1.26.0: Small changes stay small, and large requests are split before work starts. When no valid plan in state/work.md covers a request, it is sized. With no sizing signal (more than 4 points, a new data model, a new integration, several screens, or another that .mock2/capacity.md \u00a72 names) a change takes the small path: the rule it serves as one [draft] line in state/rules.md with its Given/When/Then example on the same line (or the existing rule it serves; none when behavior is unchanged), a test named after that rule, then the change, under a three-line scope record, with no mockup or interview unless asked; nothing waits for the answer to the rule's read-back. With a signal the request is classified first: FITS starts its one run, SPLIT splits the work into runs before it starts, new or changed screens get Concept and new rules get Define, in the order Concept, Define, Build, Check and Run, and a split is also shown to the user in plain words, one line per run saying what they will be able to see after it. Running /mock2-concept or /mock2-define is a request for that stage. .mock2/capacity.md \u00a72 and \u00a75 carry a worked example of each path, and the entry block, the constitution (\u00a72, \u00a712), the capacity rule and the Concept, Define and Build prompts and agents say the same. repo-init.sh now writes each template file's bytes exactly as published, downloading to a temporary file beside the target and moving it into place: before, the two Relay PNGs lost their NUL bytes and five text files gained or lost a final newline, so an upgrade took them for local edits; with --force a file is now replaced rather than written through (a symbolic link is replaced, and only the executable bit is kept), and a directory where a file goes is reported. 18 new tests (sizing.test.mjs 14, repo-init.test.mjs 4), each failing on 1.25.0; not a gate, and Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.25.0: This release includes the previously unpublished 1.24.0 session-start updater and adds opt-in parallel browser verification. The browser runner admits one group through the existing shared host budget and runs up to three isolated viewport lanes, each with one worker and ordered fixture stages. It verifies the complete planned inventory against actual results, rejects missing or retried cases, keeps mutable homes and temporary files outside sealed artifacts, waits for owned child-process cleanup and retains the serial checks. A consuming application supplies its fixture, port and data adapters before opting in; pulling the framework alone does not safely parallelize an existing suite. 1.24.0: Check first, then pull or start working. A repository brings itself up to the latest published release at the start of a session when it safely can, and otherwise starts working on the version it has. upgrade.mjs --from-site reads manifest.json and release.json from the site (5 s to a first byte, 60 s for the whole run; MOCK2_SITE or a URL names another site); when the release is newer than .mock2/standards/VERSION it downloads the release's template files into a temporary directory, checks each one's sha256 and size against release.json, and plans and applies them exactly as --from does; a file that differs or is missing, or a template manifest naming a file release.json does not list, and nothing is applied. --if-clean applies only when no tracked file has staged or unstaged changes (untracked files do not count, unless the upgrade would write one), no merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert or bisect is in progress, and the plan has no conflict and nothing only --force would replace; a path through a symbolic link is not applied. Otherwise it applies nothing, prints one line saying why (or offline: when the site does not answer) and exits 0. --commit commits exactly the paths the upgrade wrote, with installed.json, as chore(mock2): standards <from> to <to> on the current branch; it runs none of the project's hooks, never pushes, and when git refuses the commit puts every file back. The journal of a run in progress is kept in the git directory, so git add -A never commits it, and a restore after a stopped run keeps a file edited since and takes the files it added out of the index. --quiet prints only the one result line. One command on every surface: Claude Code runs it as node \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.mock2/bin/upgrade.mjs\" --from-site --if-clean --commit --quiet from the template's new .claude/settings.json (project-owned: an upgrade adds it only to a repository without one, and doctor.mjs prints the hook to add to a settings file that lacks it). Copilot's machine SessionStart hook runs it in the session's folder when that is a Mock2 repository, with the machine's upgrader when the repository's own predates 1.24.0, and its hook timeout is now 120 s. Every other agent runs it first, from the repository root, as the entry block says. A box that deploys itself may run it before its checks (.mock2/README.md). PRO current \u00a7M.9 now says what the tool does: a session-start upgrade lands as its own commit on the current branch holding only the upgrade's paths; an upgrade run by hand still goes on its own branch. The upgrader also keeps a file's mode, makes a new executable file executable, reads a CRLF checkout of a release file as that file, and keeps a CRLF entry file's line endings. 19 new tests in upgrade-site.test.mjs and upgrade-site-review.test.mjs, each failing on 1.23.3. Not a gate: with --if-clean the command always exits 0, and nothing blocks a build, a test, a commit or a push. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.3: A damaged TAR archive is incomplete coverage, never a clean pass, learned from one repository whose review found the secret scan passing a truncated archive. secrets.mjs read TAR headers up to the first empty block without checking them, so bytes that are not a TAR, a single empty block, a member that runs past the end of the file, a header with a wrong checksum or stray size digits, missing end records, or a member after the end records were reported as scanned or as no additions. It now requires whole 512-byte blocks, octal size and checksum fields, a checksum that matches (unsigned or signed), each member and its zero padding inside the file, a member name, and two end records followed only by zero blocks; anything else is reported as a damaged archive and makes coverage incomplete. Valid ustar, old-style and gzip archives and the size limits are unchanged. The file is byte for byte the repair that repository carried, so a repository holding it reads as current on upgrade; delete the secrets.mjs.mock2-new an earlier upgrade left beside it. 3 new tests in secrets-coverage.mjs, 2 failing on 1.23.2. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.2: The LOW and NIT findings of the 1.23.0 review, fixed. repo-map.mjs stamps the map with the commit (or no commits, before the first one) and a hash of the index listing (git ls-files -s) and of each tracked file that differs from the index; --check calls the map current when both match, so a map made during uncommitted work stays current until the tree changes again, one made from edits that were then discarded is stale, and before the first commit the index decides. gen.mjs appends inventory entries to the kinds that are arrays when another kind is not, names the kind it skipped and the entries to add by hand, and keeps the file's line endings (CRLF or LF). tokens.mjs --check compares with line endings normalised, so a CRLF checkout of the generated CSS is current. The reviewer agent and the check prompt follow the production checklist: a Done-when rule with no demonstration at all (neither a test nor a recorded exercise by the developer) is IN SCOPE; a rule demonstrated by a test whose name lacks its ID is a LOW FOLLOW-UP. PRO current \u00a7J.9 and the PRO rule name capabilities by their \u00a7J.4 catalog names. The template's test-changed check is no longer fast, so a repository without the check:test:changed script gets no recurring missing warning from --fast; set fast true once the script exists (.mock2/README.md). An existing repository keeps its own checks.json and removes fast there by hand if it wants to. The PRO profile says tokens.mjs writes to the output the developer names and has no default. The entry block's generators paragraph moves to Scope and the refresh hook to orientation. The 1.23.0 records count 29 generator files, state the generated branch's checks inline and cite no build-box paths or job ids. 3 new tests and 3 changed (tools), each failing on 1.23.1; 123 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.1: The six MEDIUM findings of the 1.23.0 review, fixed, each with tests that fail on 1.23.0. gen.mjs never writes through a symbolic link: a target that is one, dangling or not, or whose folder's real path is outside the repository is refused and reported, a file is created only when absent, and a Name with a path separator or .., or a --dir outside the repository, is a usage error. refresh.mjs --install-hook changes an existing hook only when it is a POSIX shell script (sh, bash, dash, zsh, or no #! line); the block goes before the first top-level command that can end the hook (exit, exec), and the report names that line; any other hook, or one the scan cannot follow, is reported as not installed and left as it was. --uninstall-hook removes exactly the bytes the install added, and a block 1.23.0 installed with the blank line before it. The repository map moves to .mock2/derived/repo-map.md, in a folder repo-map.mjs creates with a .gitignore of *, so a repository whose own .gitignore has no line for it stays clean, by hand and from the hook; a map an earlier release wrote at .mock2/repo-map.md is removed when untracked and named when committed, and the template .gitignore drops its line. tokens.mjs and refresh.mjs write CSS only to an output the developer names ($extensions.mock2.out or --out); the template's state/design/tokens.json names none, and an unedited copy of the 1.23.0 file counts the same, so the file an upgrade adds is inert and --check says no output chosen (PRO current \u00a7K.8 stays the developer's decision). The generator stops, writing no entry point, when the entry block holds a mock2 marker it does not expand. The design workflow (\u00a74) and the verification rule name the states catalog: where an app has one, its states are reviewed at /__states and its screenshots feed the visual-regression report (PRO current \u00a7K.6); optional. 8 new tests (tools 7, scoped rules 1), each failing on 1.23.0; 120 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.0: Adapted concepts: twenty-three practices from other specification, planning and build methods, each in the form that fits one agent working in sequence for a developer who speaks plain language, with nothing that blocks a build or a test. A rule in state/rules.md is stored as an EARS sentence (When, While, Where, If \u2026 then, or The system shall) with one Given/When/Then worked example, and the test that demonstrates it carries the rule's id and the example in its name; the expert answers in plain language and confirms the rule read back in plain words, and there are no BDD feature files, step definitions or runner (Define prompt, Concept agent, rules template, constitution \u00a72). An unclear point is marked where it sits, [OPEN: <question> \u2014 assuming <assumption>], work continues on the assumption, and only consequential ones become the one to three questions (capacity \u00a713). The Authorized scope record gains Known risks (rabbit holes and no-gos, drafted by the agent, confirmed by the developer) and Appetite (the developer's time, or not set); at closeout the cycle's run-ledger minutes are set beside the appetite, time over it is stated with both numbers and the developer decides; the agent may propose cuts and never makes one, and there is no circuit breaker (capacity \u00a713, the change-record rule, the reviewer agent and check prompt, constitution \u00a712). Each planned leaf item in state/work.md carries a context block (rule ids, files, decisions already made, the worked example) that later turns read instead of re-deriving (capacity \u00a74, work template). The page brief carries a breadboard (places, controls, connections) before the first render, and the mockup and state/inventory.json derive from it (design workflow \u00a73, Concept prompt, constitution \u00a72). Commit messages are typed (feat, fix, docs, test, refactor, chore, perf, build; ! for breaking) and the change record and release notes are drafted from them; nothing enforces it (change-record rule, build prompt, constitution \u00a74). Path-scoped rules: the TypeScript, phones and installed web apps, design workflow, Relay and stack/AI data rules carry a paths: header and are generated into each surface's form (Claude Code .claude/rules/mock2-*.md, Copilot .github/instructions/mock2-*.instructions.md) and into .mock2/standards/rules/SCOPES.md and the entry block's index for every other agent; capacity, change record, production checklist, run ledger, security, verification, integration and PRO apply to all work and load always. Security now loads for all work rather than only src/**. PRO current: an application lists the Base Platform capabilities it uses in app.manifest.json (name, reason, the fake for development and tests) and the host provisions from the declaration, never from inference; a use without an entry is a checklist finding (\u00a7J.9, new). Flags are an optional catalog entry with an OpenFeature-shaped interface (boolean, string and number evaluation with a default given at the call and a context, never throwing), an in-memory fake, and an owner and a removal follow-up in state/work.md per flag; added only when the developer asks, and a production value is a release decision, since commit is not release (\u00a7J.4, \u00a7J.10, new). One schema to the UI's types: the Drizzle schema gives the Zod schemas in contracts/, which the Express routes parse with and the client imports as types; nothing hand-copied, tsc in the check command reports drift (\u00a7K.7, the TypeScript rule's Express runtime section, the profile's layout). Ledger rows gain an optional last release column; a ledger without it stays valid and no id is back-filled (\u00a7I, the run-ledger rule and template). Lead time, change failure rate and time to restore are defined from releases, rollbacks and those rows, pooled, with no target, no threshold and nothing per person (\u00a7U, new). Agents read the documentation for the version the lockfile pins and name it in the change record's Docs consulted line; a scheduled host task reports outdated dependencies as small and major and proposes an S-sized follow-up the developer authorizes, with no bot pull requests, no automerge, and host forks left to their divergence record (\u00a7Q). A DTCG token file with the prefix as an output setting makes \u00a7K.8 option (b) cheap; the decision stays OPEN and option a applies. New production-checklist items for each. Derived files and generators, deterministic, Node built-ins only, nothing to install. bin/repo-map.mjs writes .mock2/repo-map.md (local, ignored): the commit hash and date, tracked files by top directory with line counts, exported symbols of JavaScript and TypeScript files, Express routes and Drizzle tables found by pattern (said to be approximate) and the state/ files, each section capped with what it left out; --check says whether the map is current (made at HEAD, no tracked change since); the entry block's orientation reads it first and refreshes it when stale. bin/trace.mjs reports the rule ids in state/rules.md that no test carries in its name or path, the inventory states the states catalog does not render, and the work items linked to no Done-when id: a short table and one JSON summary line, --json for the whole report. bin/gen.mjs runs the generators in .mock2/generators/ (list, <generator> <Name>, --dry-run, --dir), and the entry block points a standard feature shape at it: list-detail, crud and protected-route write one feature's PRO shape (a Drizzle table, its contract in contracts/, a store and its fake, one router with Zod at the boundary, a request test whose name carries a placeholder rule id, React views with data-* selectors and the feature's catalog states; protected-route answers 401 and 403 through one permission resolver), and states-catalog writes a /__states page mounted only when import.meta.env.DEV, a Playwright spec that screenshots each state and state/states-catalog.json; it never overwrites a file, appends only new entries to state/inventory.json without changing the rest, and what it writes is the project's. The generated contracts import drizzle-zod, now an optional system in stack.versions.json at 0.8.3 with its compatibility evidence (Stack-Versions.md). bin/tokens.mjs writes CSS custom properties from state/design/tokens.json (W3C DTCG: groups, $type, {alias} references, cycles and unknown references reported) with the prefix, output file and themes as settings; a dark theme applies under data-theme and prefers-color-scheme; a CSS file without the generated header is never overwritten; an output path outside the repository (absolute, with a .. segment or through a symbolic link) and a value containing a line break, ;, {, }, < or */ are refused, named and nothing is written; the template's file keeps --t- and data-theme, so \u00a7K.8 stays open with option a in force. bin/refresh.mjs runs the refreshers (tokens, catalog index, map) by hand; --install-hook adds a marked post-commit block that refreshes only the ignored map, never a tracked file, does nothing during a rebase, cherry-pick, merge, revert or bisect and always exits 0, --uninstall-hook removes only that block; opt-in. checks.json: repo-map, trace and tokens are optional report checks (resource none, fast; they exit 0 whatever they find), and test-changed (manual, fast, optional) runs the project's check:test:changed script over MOCK2_CHANGED_FILES, reported missing until the script exists, while test stays the full suite on the production checklist; the README shows a cross-platform Vitest related script. check.mjs unchanged. By upgrade an existing repository receives the managed entry block and the framework files: capacity.md, the standards and rules, the generated scoped-rule files, the five new tools and their shared helper, .mock2/generators/ and the README; state/design/tokens.json, which it does not have, is added and changes nothing until tokens.mjs runs. checks.json, .gitignore and the state templates are project-owned and do not change in an existing repository: one that wants the four new checks, the /.mock2/repo-map.md ignore line, the ledger's release column or the new rules, work and brief template text adds them by hand. Host follow-ups, with no Mock2 tool in this release: provisioning from app.manifest.json, a flag service where a host has none, the scheduled dependency task and its report, and computing and showing the delivery figures. Not added: multi-agent personas, six-week cycles and a betting table, a circuit breaker, a commit-message linter, required release-note files and automerge, agent-invoking hooks and a BDD runner. 29 new tests (tools 25, scoped rules 4), each failing on 1.22.0; 112 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.22.0: Speed and the browser harness, from one host's measurements of where a release's time goes, anonymized. A deploy and a battery no longer collide: a release prompt's step 0 checks for a running battery first, re-checks every 60 seconds for a bounded time, stops without deploying if it stays busy, and re-checks the main branch and production after any wait; a push-only release skips the wait (PRO current \u00a7P.7, new). The verification rule's browser harness retries a page navigation once when the network changed (net::ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED and its equivalents), records the retry with the state's name, and never retries an assertion or anything else; a second network change fails the state. The template ships no general browser-harness helper, so none changes (the Relay package's own parity script is unchanged). bin/check.mjs: the slowest files and the slow-file warnings read Playwright too (its list reporter's per-test times summed by file, its \"Slow test file\" lines, and a JSON report a check names in \"results\", at a path git ignores; for Playwright a file's share is of the suite's test time); --record prints the wall time, the critical path (the check that finished last), when each check finished and the slowest files. bin/doctor.mjs lists the speed settings available but off (no fast check, order config, no known-failures file, an audit not required on a lockfile change, no reuse), each as information with the setting that turns it on, and failFast as a choice; it says whether a second Mock2 block outside the markers is one an earlier template shipped. bin/upgrade.mjs: --remove-legacy-block removes such a block only when it is byte for byte one an earlier template shipped (the template manifest's legacyBlocks, which the generator recovers from the template's history), keeping the file as it was beside it; anything else is reported and left alone. --project-name fills <Project name>, and a terminal is asked for it. Standards-Benchmark.md: comparing a configuration change on the real battery (same candidate and settings, saved reports with matching settings, medians with their range; one run is a smoke test). Tests for each, failing on 1.21.0. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.21.0: The standard's name. CPR, Continuous Production Readiness, is now PRO, Production Readiness Optimization, in current text: the rules, the current baseline, the constitution, the application profile, the entry block and the entry points generated from it, the templates, the tools' messages and the manifest's titles. Citations read \"PRO current \u00a7E\". Section numbers are unchanged, so an older record's \"CPR current \u00a7E\" still finds its text. Only the name changed: the file names (CPR-v1.1-current.md, mock2-cpr.md, profile-cpr.md), the site's /core/cpr/ paths, the stored profile value cpr and the Relay package name @cpr/relay stay as they are, and the readers do not yet accept a pro profile value; a later release is to move those identifiers, readers first. The published CPR v1.1 text, its Addendum A, released versions and earlier changelog entries keep the old name for provenance, and the current baseline's header says that PRO is the standard formerly called CPR; the constitution and the PRO rule say \"formerly CPR\" where they name it. A test keeps the old name out of current text. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.20.0: Verification tooling and the rest of one host's week of review findings, anonymized. bin/check.mjs: known failures are machine-readable (.mock2/known-failures.json: check, failed count, output patterns, tracked inputs and the commit they were proven on), so a failing check every entry covers by exact figures, with its inputs unchanged since the proof, is labelled known baseline and the run's verdict is known-baselines-only (the exit status stays 1: a known failure is never a pass); one that has stood seven days or failed three runs running is named for repair. The report counts tests that never ran and test files that failed, keeps the five slowest test files and a Mock2 tool's scanned scope, and warns when one file takes most of a check or doubles. --fast runs the checks marked fast (the pre-review profile), and every check gets MOCK2_CHANGED_FILES, the files changed against the comparison base with untracked files included. scheduler.failFast (off unless set) starts no further heavy check after a required check fails or is missing for a reason no entry covers; scheduler.order history (opted into; config, the file's order, is the default) starts the checks that took longest when they last ran first, and a battery's clone gets the worktree's recent run reports for it. requiredWhenChanged makes an optional check (the dependency audit) required when its paths change, and an optional check missing three runs running is named. bin/secrets.mjs: project-declared credential shapes in .mock2/secrets.json, and text inside zip and tar archives (outgoing commits, staged, modified and untracked files) scanned as archive!/member; an archive over 64 MiB, damaged, or past a decompression limit (64 MiB an archive, 1 MiB a member) is incomplete coverage, and a Git LFS pointer or an archive the project lists in archives.exclude is reported as excluded. bin/doctor.mjs names a second, unmanaged Mock2 block outside the managed markers and a template placeholder never filled in; install.sh records ~/.mock2/installed.json; once a record exists (an unreadable one counts) it keeps a file edited since the recorded install or not from it, and removes a retired file only when it still matches its recorded hash (an